{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FV1QcKKktz6ZfoyD3eMR7o","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Gauntletdown\n2\t\n3\t*An original, Gauntlet-inspired co-op dungeon crawler for 1–4 players: original heroes, monsters, floors and voices on the my-3D2dge engine, with couch co-op on one screen and online play in AI Hub sessions.*\n4\t\n5\t**Title.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name: 50 riffs on \"Gauntlet\" were brainstormed (Gruntlet, Hauntlet, Gauntlettuce, Dauntlet, Gauntloot, Gauntlore, Gauntlathlon, ...) and one was picked with a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, the OS CSPRNG): index 30, **Gauntletdown**. It reads three ways: \"throw down the gauntlet\" (a challenge), a dungeon that only goes *down*, and a \"let-down\" for anyone who skips dinner. The whole game is built on those three readings.\n6\t\n7\t**Names (from TASK.md; if your TASK.md gives another model or timestamp, use yours):**\n8\t\n9\t| What | Name |\n10\t|---|---|\n11\t| Dev subfolder (all dev files) | `Gauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804/` |\n12\t| The deliverable (single file, in this folder's root) | `Gauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804.html` |\n13\t| Exit interview (root, beside the .html) | `exit-interview.json` |\n14\t| This plan (for \"Plan\" in Source Information) | `PLAN.md` |\n15\t\n16\t---\n17\t\n18\t## Contents\n19\t\n20\t0. Read this first\n21\t1. The game on one page\n22\t2. Deliverables and folder layout\n23\t3. Architecture\n24\t4. Systems and mechanics (the spec)\n25\t5. Content: story, heroes, bestiary, items, floors, lines\n26\t6. Build order and milestones\n27\t7. Riskiest parts and how to de-risk them\n28\t8. Testing\n29\t9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where it is met)\n30\t- Appendix A: `exit-interview.json` prefill\n31\t- Appendix B: Source Information draft\n32\t- Appendix C: Engine and hub facts this plan relies on (verified while planning)\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 0. Read this first\n37\t\n38\t- **Who this is for:** the developers (possibly other AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this plan, without the planner. Everything below is a decision, not an option, unless it says \"optional\".\n39\t- **Reading list, in order (all in the engine repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, folder `dist/handoff/`):** `README.md`; `API.md` (read whole); `AI_GUIDE.md` sections *Genre recipes* (the **Gauntlet** and **Twin-stick shooter** recipes), *Players*, *Heights*, *Bullets*, *Shell*, *Testing without a browser*, *Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep*; `kits/adventure.js` (a TileMap dungeon in `threequarter`, enemies, lighting); `kits/brawler.js` (a second player who drops in, `cam.frame`); `kits/arcade.js` and `modules/arcade.js` (`E.Arcade`: tables, initials, attract); `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes and materials). Look things up in `API-INDEX.md` with grep; don't read it whole.\n40\t- **Worth adapting from the full repository** (`src/emberdeep/`): `93-autopilot.js` (a bot driving a hero through a virtual input), `62-developer.js` (a developer panel), `30-monsters-core.js` (the `wisp` body), `31-monsters.js` (`bonethrower` lob, `imp`). Adapt, don't paste: they lean on Emberdeep's own helpers.\n41\t- **The AI Hub protocol** (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, `lib/hub/`): `README.md` (Session Protocol v1, read whole), `examples/arena.html` (the shared-world pattern this plan follows), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`, `session.js` (client 1.4.0).\n42\t- **House rules from TASK.md that shape the work:** develop in the dev subfolder; don't read files outside what you need (other agents share the machine's folders, ports and processes); prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools; when a server is needed, bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is this game before trusting a result; stop only processes you started, by PID (never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`); don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit; no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features in the shipped demo.\n43\t- **Interpretations this plan makes (so builders don't have to guess):**\n44\t  1. *\"Multiplayer: follow lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\"* In an AI Hub session the game plays in the protocol's **shared-world** mode (1–4 players, each on their own device). On its own (outside a session) it plays solo or **local couch co-op for 1–4 players on one screen**, and the hub code stays dormant, exactly as the protocol requires (\"If `connect()` answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false\"). There is no other online mode: the protocol forbids sockets or brokers of the game's own.\n45\t  2. *\"Use the engine's native style/animation as the base\"* means: the engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs, `E.MOVES`, poses, materials, props, lighting and particles, with new art and animation added where Gauntlet's identity needs it (listed in 4.22 and 4.23).\n46\t  3. *Date, Model, Environment, Usage* in Source Information come from your own TASK.md and session, not from this plan.\n47\t\n48\t---\n49\t\n50\t## 1. The game on one page\n51\t\n52\t**Core loop (one sentence, as the engine's guide asks):** descend floor by floor through a starving dungeon, blasting hordes and the generators that breed them, grabbing keys, food and treasure before your ever-draining life force runs out: together, and racing each other for score.\n53\t\n54\t**Genre:** arcade co-op dungeon crawler: a top-down hack-and-shoot (\"hack-and-slash shooter\") with maze exploration. Catalogue genre: **Action**.\n55\t**Perspective:** **three-quarter overhead** (the engine's `threequarter` view, 55° pitch): it shows wall faces and character faces (the N64-era *Gauntlet Legends* angle) while keeping the maze readable. **Top-down** (`topdown`, 80°) and **isometric** (`iso`) are selectable in Settings. The rules are the same in every view.\n56\t**Players:** 1–4. Couch co-op with drop-in and drop-out (gamepads, and up to two players on one keyboard); online 1–4 through AI Hub sessions.\n57\t**Session length:** a full descent is about 35–50 minutes (9 floors, 2 treasure rooms, a boss); an AI Hub round is 6 minutes.\n58\t\n59\t### What makes Gauntlet distinctive, and how this game captures it\n60\t\n61\t| Source trait | In Gauntletdown |\n62\t|---|---|\n63\t| Four archetype heroes with clearly different stats, up to four at once | Berserker, Shieldmaiden, Ranger, Hexer (original characters), each with its own armor, shot, melee, magic and speed; 1–4 players |\n64\t| Health ticks down every second; food is life | **Life force** drains 1 per second; roasts, loaves and cider restore it; health is shown as a big number, like the arcade |\n65\t| Endless hordes from generators | Six generator types breed monsters until destroyed; crowds jam corridors |\n66\t| Monsters come in strength levels and weaken when hit | Ranks 3, 2, 1: a wounded rank-3 monster visibly shrinks to rank 2, then 1 |\n67\t| Keys open doors; magic potions clear the screen | A key opens a whole run of doors; a **Blast Flask** strikes every monster on screen |\n68\t| \"Don't shoot the food\"; shooting a potion wastes it | Cider and stew can be shot (and are destroyed); a shot flask detonates at half power |\n69\t| Death: can't be shot, drains your health, only magic kills it | **The Tithe**, a reaper that drains up to 200 and only magic can kill |\n70\t| The booming announcer (\"... needs food badly\", \"... shot the food\") | **The Gaunt**, the dungeon's own voice: terse booming callouts in the same style, with original lines, spoken in deep gibberish under big captions |\n71\t| Exits, skip-exits, traps, transporters, treasure rooms, stun tiles, force fields | All present (4.8) |\n72\t| Co-op that is also competitive | Individual scores; whoever takes the stairs gets the exit bonus; AI Hub standings are by score |\n73\t| Insert-a-coin drop-in, a loop that never ends | Join any time; fallen heroes **rise again**; after the ending, **Descent II** loops the dungeon harder |\n74\t| Walls turn into exits if you dawdle | The Gaunt grows impatient: doors open at 3:00, every wall becomes a stair at 4:00 |\n75\t\n76\t### Originality guardrails (non-negotiable)\n77\t\n78\tBuild an original game *in the genre* of Gauntlet, not a recreation of it (TASK.md and the system rules):\n79\t- No names, characters, art, music, levels or exact lines from any Gauntlet game. Not Thor, Thyra, Merlin or Questor; not \"Warrior/Valkyrie/Wizard/Elf\" as the class set; not \"Wizard needs food, badly\", \"Elf shot the food\", \"Remember, don't shoot food\", or \"Shots do not hurt other players... yet\". The lines in 5.6 are original and follow the source's *style*: short, booming, third-person, darkly funny.\n80\t- All floors are this plan's own layouts. All music is original composition. Monster designs are this plan's (hunger-themed: ghosts with gaping mouths, kitchen imps, frying-pan friars).\n81\t- The title is a riff on the source's name, as TASK.md requires.\n82\t\n83\t---\n84\t\n85\t## 2. Deliverables and folder layout\n86\t\n87\t### The run folder (this folder)\n88\t\n89\t```\n90\tPLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md                              (given)\n91\tGauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804.html   <- the game, one file, works from file://\n92\texit-interview.json                                      <- written last (Appendix A), must parse as JSON\n93\tGauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804/      <- every dev file\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\t### The dev subfolder\n97\t\n98\tStart it as a copy of the engine's `dist/handoff/` (sparse-clone it: `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, and copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev subfolder), so the engine's tools work with their own paths. Then add:\n99\t\n100\t```\n101\t<dev>/\n102\t  my-3d2dge.js          the full engine (every module included), PATCHED as listed in 3.5\n103\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md     each engine change: what, why, its test (feeds Engine Feedback)\n104\t  API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/, tools/, src/assets/ (from the handoff: references, tools, the asset store)\n105\t  package.json          from the handoff (pins Playwright 1.63.0); `npm install` once\n106\t  hub/                  session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md, fetched with\n107\t                        curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>\n108\t  game/\n109\t    src/NN-*.js         the game's source fragments (3.2), concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n110\t    game.js             built from src/ (never edited by hand)\n111\t    index.html          the dev page: session.js first in <head>, then my-3d2dge.js, then game.js\n112\t  build.mjs             builds game/game.js and the single-file deliverable (3.6)\n113\t  tests/                run.mjs (the one command) and the suites (section 8)\n114\t  docs/DESIGNER.md      how to add a floor, a monster, an item or a line (the \"designer tools\" note)\n115\t  shots/                screenshots for look review (not shipped)\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\tThe handoff's template game goes: `game/game.js` is overwritten by `build.mjs`, and `game/test.mjs` (and `kits/`, once you have taken what you need) can be deleted. The deliverable is only the single HTML file; the dev folder is the workshop.\n119\t\n120\t### What the finished demo contains\n121\t\n122\tTitle screen with attract mode; main menu; class select; How to Play; High Scores; About/Credits (with the four feedback sections); 9 floors in three realms, 2 treasure rooms and a boss; an ending, credits and the Descent II loop; game over, continue and initials entry; the engine's pause menu, settings and controls (adjusted), plus **Dev Tools**; local co-op 1–4; AI Hub shared-world sessions 1–4; the touch-only notice. Audio entirely synthesized in code.\n123\t\n124\t---\n125\t\n126\t## 3. Architecture\n127\t\n128\t### 3.1 Base\n129\t\n130\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, which already includes every module: `arcade`, `grid`, `gen`, ...). Plain Canvas 2D at a fixed low resolution, procedural rigs, chip-synth audio, optional WebGPU lighting. No other libraries, no CDN, no network (which also satisfies the hub's network rule).\n131\t- **Start point:** the engine's Gauntlet recipe (*AI_GUIDE.md*): \"Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a TileMap dungeon in threequarter or topdown); up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors; generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero, drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }`.\" Take the adventure kit's TileMap, floor texture, lighting and enemy patterns; take the brawler kit's drop-in; take `E.Arcade` for tables and initials. Write the game itself fresh in the module layout below. Don't keep the kit's starter shell or its slices.\n132\t- **Display:** `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'threequarter', views: ['threequarter', 'topdown', 'iso'], input: GD_INPUT, id: 'gauntletdown', menu: {...} })`. `expand: true` fills any screen: a 16:9 TV shows about 427 × 240 game pixels, scaled about 4.5× at 1080p, so the 5×7 pixel font is about 32 px tall on a TV, readable at 10 feet. Every HUD element anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n133\t\n134\t### 3.2 Modules (`game/src/`, concatenated in name order inside one IIFE)\n135\t\n136\t| File | Responsibility |\n137\t|---|---|\n138\t| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; constants (`T = 16`); `SESSION = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)`; the `new E.Game(...)` call (session and normal options differ, 4.20); registries `def(kind, id, spec)` / `get(kind, id)` / `all(kind)`; the event bus `bus.on(name, fn)` / `bus.emit(name, data)`; seeded RNG streams (`rng.sim`, `rng.fx`) |\n139\t| `01-tune.js` | `TUNE`: every balance number in one table (4.24), with defaults, ranges and labels; difficulty and player-count scaling; the Dev sliders bind here |\n140\t| `05-text.js` | All player-facing text: narrator lines (5.6), story (5.1), floor intros, How to Play cards, class quips, About/Credits pages (Source Information and feedback filled in at the end) |\n141\t| `10-heroes.js` | `def('heroes', ...)` ×4: stats, rig factory, shot, melee moves, magic power, voice |\n142\t| `11-monsters.js` | `def('monsters', ...)` ×7 and `def('boss', 'famish', ...)`: stats per rank, AI kind, rig factory, generator kind, score |\n143\t| `12-items.js` | `def('items', ...)` for every pickup, 7 special potions, 5 amulets: art, effect, shootable or not, points |\n144\t| `13-art.js` | New art: string sprites, `E.SHAPES` entries (generators with damage states, doors, stairs, plates, pads, runes, pylons, bell, jug, platter, tureen), the `Shade` ghost rig class, realm palettes and materials |\n145\t| `15..18-floors-*.js` | `def('floors', ...)`: rows and meta for each floor (5.5), one file per realm plus the banquet |\n146\t| `20-level.js` | Floor parser (legend → TileMap, entity lists, door groups, trap groups, transporter pairs, exits, beams); automatic decoration (torches, props) from a hash; lights list; **`validateFloor()`**, shared with the tests |\n147\t| `21-sim.js` | The world (`W`): state, step order, damage pipeline, scoring, kill and pickup events |\n148\t| `22-sim-heroes.js` | Hero controller: intent → movement, aim, fire, smart melee, plant, magic; drain; pickups; doors; stairs; death and rising; amulets and specials |\n149\t| `23-sim-monsters.js` | Monster AI kinds, flow fields (multi-source and single-source), separation, ranks |\n150\t| `24-sim-generators.js` | Spawning, ranks, damage states, caps |\n151\t| `25-sim-features.js` | Traps, transporters, stun runes, force fields, spikes, cracked and fake walls, impatience, treasure-room clock |\n152\t| `26-sim-boss.js` | King Famish's fight as an `E.Script` (phases, attacks, waves, feast) |\n153\t| `30-intents.js` | Intents from a local player's input (keyboard, mouse, gamepad), from the network, from the bot |\n154\t| `31-bot.js` | The autopilot (attract mode, playthrough tests, Dev \"bot plays P1\") |\n155\t| `40-net.js` | Net roles: local, host, client, server; snapshot and event encoding; client puppets and interpolation; rate limiting; area of interest |\n156\t| `41-session.js` | The AI Hub flow: connect, own card, ready, start countdown, time-up, finish, end, leave |\n157\t| `50-view.js` | Drawing: floor and walls, decoration, lights, entities (from the sim or from puppets), projectiles, reticle, x-ray, debug overlays |\n158\t| `51-hud.js` | Player panels, captions, floor and realm cards, timers, edge arrows, prompts |\n159\t| `52-narrator.js` | The callout queue: priority, cooldowns, captions, voice |\n160\t| `53-audio.js` | Songs, sound-effect definitions, the **Babble** voice synth, dynamic music layers |\n161\t| `60-scenes-front.js` | Title, main menu, class select, How to Play, High Scores, About/Credits, the touch-only notice |\n162\t| `61-scenes-play.js` | Play (solo and local co-op), floor cards, treasure rooms, game over, continue, initials, ending, credits, Descent II |\n163\t| `62-scenes-session.js` | Session scenes: card, countdown, play, round over, server |\n164\t| `63-attract.js` | The attract-mode director |\n165\t| `70-dev.js` | Dev Tools: cheats, warp, give and spawn, tuning sliders, overlays |\n166\t| `80-debug.js` | `game.debug` (snapshot, teleport, warp, give, spawn, setTune, bot, netStats) and `game.stateHash` |\n167\t| `99-start.js` | `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })` |\n168\t\n169\tContent never edits the core: floors, monsters, items and lines are registry entries (the Emberdeep lesson), so the rest of the game can be built later by adding data in a text editor (`docs/DESIGNER.md` explains how).\n170\t\n171\t### 3.3 Runtime model: simulation, intents, view, network\n172\t\n173\t```\n174\t           +---------------- intents ----------------+\n175\t local input (per player) --+                         |\n176\t network 'me' messages -----+--> Hero controllers --> |  SIM (world W: heroes, monsters, generators,\n177\t bot (autopilot) -----------+                         |  items, projectiles, doors, timers, boss, score)\n178\t                                                      |        |  events (bus): hit, kill, pickup, door,\n179\t                                                      |        |  say, floor, magic, over ...\n180\t                                                      v        v\n181\t             VIEW (draw from W, or from puppets) <-- NET: local | host (snapshots+events out) | client (in)\n182\t                     HUD, narrator, audio, effects listen to the bus\n183\t```\n184\t\n185\t- **One simulation, one place.** The sim runs in `solo`, `local`, `attract`, `host` and `server` roles. A `client` runs no monster AI, no generators and no damage; it moves only its own hero (with the same controller and collision, for zero-lag control) and shows everything else from the host's snapshots.\n186\t- **Intents** are the only way anything drives a hero: `{ move: [x, y] (ground, length ≤ 1), aim: angle or null, fire: held, magic: pressed, plant: held }`. Local input, network messages and the bot all produce intents. This is what makes the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the netcode the same code path.\n187\t- **Entity ids:** every entity has an integer id. Static floor items get ids in reading order of the rows, so every copy derives the same ids from the same data and only removals travel over the network. Dynamic entities (monsters, drops, projectiles) take ids from the host's counter.\n188\t- **Damage pipeline (one function for everything):** `damage(target, amount, { by, kind: 'shot'|'melee'|'magic'|'blast'|'contact'|'drain'|'trap'|'fire' })` applies armor and immunities, recomputes monster rank, handles death, awards score and emits `hit`, `kill`, `hurt`.\n189\t- **Step order (each 1/120 s):** intents → heroes → generators → monsters → projectiles → features (traps, fields, timers) → pickups and doors → stairs → narrator triggers → net send. The view reads the result in `draw`.\n190\t- **Randomness:** gameplay draws from `rng.sim` (`E.rng(seed)`), cosmetic effects from `Math.random`. Tests seed both, so runs repeat exactly.\n191\t\n192\t### 3.4 Scenes and flow\n193\t\n194\t```\n195\tboot -> title --(10 s idle)--> attract (loops: demo floors -> high scores -> prologue -> title card) --(any input)--> title\n196\ttitle -> main menu: START DESCENT | HOW TO PLAY | HIGH SCORES | SETTINGS | ABOUT & CREDITS\n197\tSTART -> class select -> floor card -> play <-> pause menu (Resume, How to Play, Dev Tools, Drop out, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit)\n198\tplay -> floor card -> ... treasure room after floors 3 and 6 ... -> boss -> ending -> credits -> DESCENT II or title\n199\tall heroes down -> continue (10 s) -> yes: play | no: game over -> initials (each qualifying player) -> high scores -> title\n200\tAI Hub session: boot -> session card -> countdown 3-2-1 -> session play -> round over (stays until the hub removes the game)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"e0b2f12e-f355-4402-9f07-7b44a3c2412b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:02:56.749Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build__20261104-050244__x8ffc89/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Gauntletdown\n\n*An original, Gauntlet-inspired co-op dungeon crawler for 1–4 players: original heroes, monsters, floors and voices on the my-3D2dge engine, with couch co-op on one screen and online play in AI Hub sessions.*\n\n**Title.** TASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name: 50 riffs on \"Gauntlet\" were brainstormed (Gruntlet, Hauntlet, Gauntlettuce, Dauntlet, Gauntloot, Gauntlore, Gauntlathlon, ...) and one was picked with a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, the OS CSPRNG): index 30, **Gauntletdown**. It reads three ways: \"throw down the gauntlet\" (a challenge), a dungeon that only goes *down*, and a \"let-down\" for anyone who skips dinner. The whole game is built on those three readings.\n\n**Names (from TASK.md; if your TASK.md gives another model or timestamp, use yours):**\n\n| What | Name |\n|---|---|\n| Dev subfolder (all dev files) | `Gauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804/` |\n| The deliverable (single file, in this folder's root) | `Gauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804.html` |\n| Exit interview (root, beside the .html) | `exit-interview.json` |\n| This plan (for \"Plan\" in Source Information) | `PLAN.md` |\n\n---\n\n## Contents\n\n0. Read this first\n1. The game on one page\n2. Deliverables and folder layout\n3. Architecture\n4. Systems and mechanics (the spec)\n5. Content: story, heroes, bestiary, items, floors, lines\n6. Build order and milestones\n7. Riskiest parts and how to de-risk them\n8. Testing\n9. Done checklist (every TASK.md requirement, and where it is met)\n- Appendix A: `exit-interview.json` prefill\n- Appendix B: Source Information draft\n- Appendix C: Engine and hub facts this plan relies on (verified while planning)\n\n---\n\n## 0. Read this first\n\n- **Who this is for:** the developers (possibly other AI models) who build the game from TASK.md and this plan, without the planner. Everything below is a decision, not an option, unless it says \"optional\".\n- **Reading list, in order (all in the engine repository, github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge, folder `dist/handoff/`):** `README.md`; `API.md` (read whole); `AI_GUIDE.md` sections *Genre recipes* (the **Gauntlet** and **Twin-stick shooter** recipes), *Players*, *Heights*, *Bullets*, *Shell*, *Testing without a browser*, *Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep*; `kits/adventure.js` (a TileMap dungeon in `threequarter`, enemies, lighting); `kits/brawler.js` (a second player who drops in, `cam.frame`); `kits/arcade.js` and `modules/arcade.js` (`E.Arcade`: tables, initials, attract); `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes and materials). Look things up in `API-INDEX.md` with grep; don't read it whole.\n- **Worth adapting from the full repository** (`src/emberdeep/`): `93-autopilot.js` (a bot driving a hero through a virtual input), `62-developer.js` (a developer panel), `30-monsters-core.js` (the `wisp` body), `31-monsters.js` (`bonethrower` lob, `imp`). Adapt, don't paste: they lean on Emberdeep's own helpers.\n- **The AI Hub protocol** (github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub, `lib/hub/`): `README.md` (Session Protocol v1, read whole), `examples/arena.html` (the shared-world pattern this plan follows), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js`, `session.js` (client 1.4.0).\n- **House rules from TASK.md that shape the work:** develop in the dev subfolder; don't read files outside what you need (other agents share the machine's folders, ports and processes); prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools; when a server is needed, bind `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks and confirm the page is this game before trusting a result; stop only processes you started, by PID (never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`); don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit; no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features in the shipped demo.\n- **Interpretations this plan makes (so builders don't have to guess):**\n  1. *\"Multiplayer: follow lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\"* In an AI Hub session the game plays in the protocol's **shared-world** mode (1–4 players, each on their own device). On its own (outside a session) it plays solo or **local couch co-op for 1–4 players on one screen**, and the hub code stays dormant, exactly as the protocol requires (\"If `connect()` answers `null`, carry on as when `AIHub.active` is false\"). There is no other online mode: the protocol forbids sockets or brokers of the game's own.\n  2. *\"Use the engine's native style/animation as the base\"* means: the engine's procedural Humanoid and Blob rigs, `E.MOVES`, poses, materials, props, lighting and particles, with new art and animation added where Gauntlet's identity needs it (listed in 4.22 and 4.23).\n  3. *Date, Model, Environment, Usage* in Source Information come from your own TASK.md and session, not from this plan.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, as the engine's guide asks):** descend floor by floor through a starving dungeon, blasting hordes and the generators that breed them, grabbing keys, food and treasure before your ever-draining life force runs out: together, and racing each other for score.\n\n**Genre:** arcade co-op dungeon crawler: a top-down hack-and-shoot (\"hack-and-slash shooter\") with maze exploration. Catalogue genre: **Action**.\n**Perspective:** **three-quarter overhead** (the engine's `threequarter` view, 55° pitch): it shows wall faces and character faces (the N64-era *Gauntlet Legends* angle) while keeping the maze readable. **Top-down** (`topdown`, 80°) and **isometric** (`iso`) are selectable in Settings. The rules are the same in every view.\n**Players:** 1–4. Couch co-op with drop-in and drop-out (gamepads, and up to two players on one keyboard); online 1–4 through AI Hub sessions.\n**Session length:** a full descent is about 35–50 minutes (9 floors, 2 treasure rooms, a boss); an AI Hub round is 6 minutes.\n\n### What makes Gauntlet distinctive, and how this game captures it\n\n| Source trait | In Gauntletdown |\n|---|---|\n| Four archetype heroes with clearly different stats, up to four at once | Berserker, Shieldmaiden, Ranger, Hexer (original characters), each with its own armor, shot, melee, magic and speed; 1–4 players |\n| Health ticks down every second; food is life | **Life force** drains 1 per second; roasts, loaves and cider restore it; health is shown as a big number, like the arcade |\n| Endless hordes from generators | Six generator types breed monsters until destroyed; crowds jam corridors |\n| Monsters come in strength levels and weaken when hit | Ranks 3, 2, 1: a wounded rank-3 monster visibly shrinks to rank 2, then 1 |\n| Keys open doors; magic potions clear the screen | A key opens a whole run of doors; a **Blast Flask** strikes every monster on screen |\n| \"Don't shoot the food\"; shooting a potion wastes it | Cider and stew can be shot (and are destroyed); a shot flask detonates at half power |\n| Death: can't be shot, drains your health, only magic kills it | **The Tithe**, a reaper that drains up to 200 and only magic can kill |\n| The booming announcer (\"... needs food badly\", \"... shot the food\") | **The Gaunt**, the dungeon's own voice: terse booming callouts in the same style, with original lines, spoken in deep gibberish under big captions |\n| Exits, skip-exits, traps, transporters, treasure rooms, stun tiles, force fields | All present (4.8) |\n| Co-op that is also competitive | Individual scores; whoever takes the stairs gets the exit bonus; AI Hub standings are by score |\n| Insert-a-coin drop-in, a loop that never ends | Join any time; fallen heroes **rise again**; after the ending, **Descent II** loops the dungeon harder |\n| Walls turn into exits if you dawdle | The Gaunt grows impatient: doors open at 3:00, every wall becomes a stair at 4:00 |\n\n### Originality guardrails (non-negotiable)\n\nBuild an original game *in the genre* of Gauntlet, not a recreation of it (TASK.md and the system rules):\n- No names, characters, art, music, levels or exact lines from any Gauntlet game. Not Thor, Thyra, Merlin or Questor; not \"Warrior/Valkyrie/Wizard/Elf\" as the class set; not \"Wizard needs food, badly\", \"Elf shot the food\", \"Remember, don't shoot food\", or \"Shots do not hurt other players... yet\". The lines in 5.6 are original and follow the source's *style*: short, booming, third-person, darkly funny.\n- All floors are this plan's own layouts. All music is original composition. Monster designs are this plan's (hunger-themed: ghosts with gaping mouths, kitchen imps, frying-pan friars).\n- The title is a riff on the source's name, as TASK.md requires.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Deliverables and folder layout\n\n### The run folder (this folder)\n\n```\nPLAN.md, STAGE.md, TASK.md                              (given)\nGauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804.html   <- the game, one file, works from file://\nexit-interview.json                                      <- written last (Appendix A), must parse as JSON\nGauntletdown-Gauntlet-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174804/      <- every dev file\n```\n\n### The dev subfolder\n\nStart it as a copy of the engine's `dist/handoff/` (sparse-clone it: `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` then `git sparse-checkout set dist/handoff`, and copy `dist/handoff/*` into the dev subfolder), so the engine's tools work with their own paths. Then add:\n\n```\n<dev>/\n  my-3d2dge.js          the full engine (every module included), PATCHED as listed in 3.5\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md     each engine change: what, why, its test (feeds Engine Feedback)\n  API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/, tools/, src/assets/ (from the handoff: references, tools, the asset store)\n  package.json          from the handoff (pins Playwright 1.63.0); `npm install` once\n  hub/                  session.js, harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md, fetched with\n                        curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>\n  game/\n    src/NN-*.js         the game's source fragments (3.2), concatenated in name order into one IIFE\n    game.js             built from src/ (never edited by hand)\n    index.html          the dev page: session.js first in <head>, then my-3d2dge.js, then game.js\n  build.mjs             builds game/game.js and the single-file deliverable (3.6)\n  tests/                run.mjs (the one command) and the suites (section 8)\n  docs/DESIGNER.md      how to add a floor, a monster, an item or a line (the \"designer tools\" note)\n  shots/                screenshots for look review (not shipped)\n```\n\nThe handoff's template game goes: `game/game.js` is overwritten by `build.mjs`, and `game/test.mjs` (and `kits/`, once you have taken what you need) can be deleted. The deliverable is only the single HTML file; the dev folder is the workshop.\n\n### What the finished demo contains\n\nTitle screen with attract mode; main menu; class select; How to Play; High Scores; About/Credits (with the four feedback sections); 9 floors in three realms, 2 treasure rooms and a boss; an ending, credits and the Descent II loop; game over, continue and initials entry; the engine's pause menu, settings and controls (adjusted), plus **Dev Tools**; local co-op 1–4; AI Hub shared-world sessions 1–4; the touch-only notice. Audio entirely synthesized in code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Base\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge **v0.85.2**, the full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, which already includes every module: `arcade`, `grid`, `gen`, ...). Plain Canvas 2D at a fixed low resolution, procedural rigs, chip-synth audio, optional WebGPU lighting. No other libraries, no CDN, no network (which also satisfies the hub's network rule).\n- **Start point:** the engine's Gauntlet recipe (*AI_GUIDE.md*): \"Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a TileMap dungeon in threequarter or topdown); up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors; generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero, drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }`.\" Take the adventure kit's TileMap, floor texture, lighting and enemy patterns; take the brawler kit's drop-in; take `E.Arcade` for tables and initials. Write the game itself fresh in the module layout below. Don't keep the kit's starter shell or its slices.\n- **Display:** `new E.Game({ canvas: 'screen', res: 'ps1', expand: true, view: 'threequarter', views: ['threequarter', 'topdown', 'iso'], input: GD_INPUT, id: 'gauntletdown', menu: {...} })`. `expand: true` fills any screen: a 16:9 TV shows about 427 × 240 game pixels, scaled about 4.5× at 1080p, so the 5×7 pixel font is about 32 px tall on a TV, readable at 10 feet. Every HUD element anchors to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n\n### 3.2 Modules (`game/src/`, concatenated in name order inside one IIFE)\n\n| File | Responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `const E = My3D2dge`; constants (`T = 16`); `SESSION = !!(window.AIHub && AIHub.active)`; the `new E.Game(...)` call (session and normal options differ, 4.20); registries `def(kind, id, spec)` / `get(kind, id)` / `all(kind)`; the event bus `bus.on(name, fn)` / `bus.emit(name, data)`; seeded RNG streams (`rng.sim`, `rng.fx`) |\n| `01-tune.js` | `TUNE`: every balance number in one table (4.24), with defaults, ranges and labels; difficulty and player-count scaling; the Dev sliders bind here |\n| `05-text.js` | All player-facing text: narrator lines (5.6), story (5.1), floor intros, How to Play cards, class quips, About/Credits pages (Source Information and feedback filled in at the end) |\n| `10-heroes.js` | `def('heroes', ...)` ×4: stats, rig factory, shot, melee moves, magic power, voice |\n| `11-monsters.js` | `def('monsters', ...)` ×7 and `def('boss', 'famish', ...)`: stats per rank, AI kind, rig factory, generator kind, score |\n| `12-items.js` | `def('items', ...)` for every pickup, 7 special potions, 5 amulets: art, effect, shootable or not, points |\n| `13-art.js` | New art: string sprites, `E.SHAPES` entries (generators with damage states, doors, stairs, plates, pads, runes, pylons, bell, jug, platter, tureen), the `Shade` ghost rig class, realm palettes and materials |\n| `15..18-floors-*.js` | `def('floors', ...)`: rows and meta for each floor (5.5), one file per realm plus the banquet |\n| `20-level.js` | Floor parser (legend → TileMap, entity lists, door groups, trap groups, transporter pairs, exits, beams); automatic decoration (torches, props) from a hash; lights list; **`validateFloor()`**, shared with the tests |\n| `21-sim.js` | The world (`W`): state, step order, damage pipeline, scoring, kill and pickup events |\n| `22-sim-heroes.js` | Hero controller: intent → movement, aim, fire, smart melee, plant, magic; drain; pickups; doors; stairs; death and rising; amulets and specials |\n| `23-sim-monsters.js` | Monster AI kinds, flow fields (multi-source and single-source), separation, ranks |\n| `24-sim-generators.js` | Spawning, ranks, damage states, caps |\n| `25-sim-features.js` | Traps, transporters, stun runes, force fields, spikes, cracked and fake walls, impatience, treasure-room clock |\n| `26-sim-boss.js` | King Famish's fight as an `E.Script` (phases, attacks, waves, feast) |\n| `30-intents.js` | Intents from a local player's input (keyboard, mouse, gamepad), from the network, from the bot |\n| `31-bot.js` | The autopilot (attract mode, playthrough tests, Dev \"bot plays P1\") |\n| `40-net.js` | Net roles: local, host, client, server; snapshot and event encoding; client puppets and interpolation; rate limiting; area of interest |\n| `41-session.js` | The AI Hub flow: connect, own card, ready, start countdown, time-up, finish, end, leave |\n| `50-view.js` | Drawing: floor and walls, decoration, lights, entities (from the sim or from puppets), projectiles, reticle, x-ray, debug overlays |\n| `51-hud.js` | Player panels, captions, floor and realm cards, timers, edge arrows, prompts |\n| `52-narrator.js` | The callout queue: priority, cooldowns, captions, voice |\n| `53-audio.js` | Songs, sound-effect definitions, the **Babble** voice synth, dynamic music layers |\n| `60-scenes-front.js` | Title, main menu, class select, How to Play, High Scores, About/Credits, the touch-only notice |\n| `61-scenes-play.js` | Play (solo and local co-op), floor cards, treasure rooms, game over, continue, initials, ending, credits, Descent II |\n| `62-scenes-session.js` | Session scenes: card, countdown, play, round over, server |\n| `63-attract.js` | The attract-mode director |\n| `70-dev.js` | Dev Tools: cheats, warp, give and spawn, tuning sliders, overlays |\n| `80-debug.js` | `game.debug` (snapshot, teleport, warp, give, spawn, setTune, bot, netStats) and `game.stateHash` |\n| `99-start.js` | `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot })` |\n\nContent never edits the core: floors, monsters, items and lines are registry entries (the Emberdeep lesson), so the rest of the game can be built later by adding data in a text editor (`docs/DESIGNER.md` explains how).\n\n### 3.3 Runtime model: simulation, intents, view, network\n\n```\n           +---------------- intents ----------------+\n local input (per player) --+                         |\n network 'me' messages -----+--> Hero controllers --> |  SIM (world W: heroes, monsters, generators,\n bot (autopilot) -----------+                         |  items, projectiles, doors, timers, boss, score)\n                                                      |        |  events (bus): hit, kill, pickup, door,\n                                                      |        |  say, floor, magic, over ...\n                                                      v        v\n             VIEW (draw from W, or from puppets) <-- NET: local | host (snapshots+events out) | client (in)\n                     HUD, narrator, audio, effects listen to the bus\n```\n\n- **One simulation, one place.** The sim runs in `solo`, `local`, `attract`, `host` and `server` roles. A `client` runs no monster AI, no generators and no damage; it moves only its own hero (with the same controller and collision, for zero-lag control) and shows everything else from the host's snapshots.\n- **Intents** are the only way anything drives a hero: `{ move: [x, y] (ground, length ≤ 1), aim: angle or null, fire: held, magic: pressed, plant: held }`. Local input, network messages and the bot all produce intents. This is what makes the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the netcode the same code path.\n- **Entity ids:** every entity has an integer id. Static floor items get ids in reading order of the rows, so every copy derives the same ids from the same data and only removals travel over the network. Dynamic entities (monsters, drops, projectiles) take ids from the host's counter.\n- **Damage pipeline (one function for everything):** `damage(target, amount, { by, kind: 'shot'|'melee'|'magic'|'blast'|'contact'|'drain'|'trap'|'fire' })` applies armor and immunities, recomputes monster rank, handles death, awards score and emits `hit`, `kill`, `hurt`.\n- **Step order (each 1/120 s):** intents → heroes → generators → monsters → projectiles → features (traps, fields, timers) → pickups and doors → stairs → narrator triggers → net send. The view reads the result in `draw`.\n- **Randomness:** gameplay draws from `rng.sim` (`E.rng(seed)`), cosmetic effects from `Math.random`. Tests seed both, so runs repeat exactly.\n\n### 3.4 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot -> title --(10 s idle)--> attract (loops: demo floors -> high scores -> prologue -> title card) --(any input)--> title\ntitle -> main menu: START DESCENT | HOW TO PLAY | HIGH SCORES | SETTINGS | ABOUT & CREDITS\nSTART -> class select -> floor card -> play <-> pause menu (Resume, How to Play, Dev Tools, Drop out, Settings, Controls, Full screen, Quit)\nplay -> floor card -> ... treasure room after floors 3 and 6 ... -> boss -> ending -> credits -> DESCENT II or title\nall heroes down -> continue (10 s) -> yes: play | no: game over -> initials (each qualifying player) -> high scores -> title\nAI Hub session: boot -> session card -> countdown 3-2-1 -> session play -> round over (stays until the hub removes the game)","numLines":200,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1028}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FV1QcKKktz6ZfoyD3eMR7o","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
