{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrcFK9m2BCZwwCgZ4es","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UGrfiJh34yBZJ8Hmoafg83","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261104-050244--x8ffc89-work/9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094/tool-results/bxit8s57l.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1024,"cache_read_input_tokens":124146,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1024},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"df92769c-d91c-47ae-8d9c-2dc19bfdd738","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:15.431Z","request_id":"req_011CghrcF51aKYdEcs8jPXD6","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UGrfiJh34yBZJ8Hmoafg83":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261104-050244--x8ffc89-work/9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094/tool-results/bxit8s57l.txt"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UGrfiJh34yBZJ8Hmoafg83","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Systems in more depth\n2\t\n3\t### Views and resolution\n4\t- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n5\t- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n6\t- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n7\t- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n8\t- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n9\t- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n10\t\n11\t### Scenes\n12\tA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n13\t- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n14\t- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n15\t\n16\t### Camera\n17\t- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n18\t- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n19\t- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n20\t- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n21\t- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n22\t- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n23\t\n24\t### Players (two to four on one machine)\n25\t- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n26\t- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n27\t- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n28\t- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n29\t\n30\t### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\n31\tThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n32\t- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n33\t- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n34\t- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n35\t- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n36\t- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n37\t- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n38\t\n39\t### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n40\t- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n41\t- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n42\t- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n43\t- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n44\t- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n45\t- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n46\t- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n47\t- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n48\t- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n49\t- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n50\t\n51\t### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\n52\tOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n53\t- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n54\t- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n55\t- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n56\t- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n57\t\n58\tThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n59\t- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n60\t- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n61\t- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n62\t- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n63\t- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n64\t- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n65\t\n66\tReach and perception:\n67\t- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n68\t- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n69\t\n70\tNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n71\t\n72\t```js\n73\tconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\n74\tif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n75\t  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n76\t  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n77\t  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n78\t}\n79\tconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\n80\tif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\t### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n84\t- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n85\t- Kinds:\n86\t  - `solid`;\n87\t  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n88\t  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n89\t  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n90\t  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n91\t  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n92\t  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n93\t- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n94\t- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n95\t- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n96\t  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n97\t  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n98\t  - `hitCeiling`;\n99\t  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n100\t  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n101\t- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n102\t\n103\t### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n104\t- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n105\t- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n106\t- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n107\t- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n108\t- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n109\t\n110\t### Body (ground-plane physics)\n111\t- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n112\t- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n113\t- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n114\t- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n115\t\n116\t### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n117\t`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n118\t- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n119\t- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n120\t- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n121\t- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n122\t- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n123\t- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n124\t- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n125\t- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n126\t- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n127\t\n128\t### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n129\t- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n130\t- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n131\t- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n132\t- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n133\t\n134\t### Attack (melee timing)\n135\t- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n136\t- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n137\t- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n138\t- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n139\t- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n140\t\n141\t### Bullets and patterns\n142\t- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n143\t- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n144\t- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n145\t- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n146\t\n147\t### Humanoid\n148\t- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n149\t- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n150\t- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n151\t- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n152\t- Attacks:\n153\t  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n154\t  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n155\t  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n156\t- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n157\t\n158\t### Audio\n159\t- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n160\t- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n161\t- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n162\t- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n163\t- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n164\t- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n165\t\n166\t### Text and UI\n167\t- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n168\t- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n169\t- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n170\t- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n171\t\n172\t### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n173\tA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n174\t- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n175\t- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n176\t- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n177\t- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n178\t- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n179\t\n180\t### Lighting\n181\t- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n182\t- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n183\t  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n184\t  - light that wraps around characters;\n185\t  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n186\t  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n187\t- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n188\t\n189\t### Errors and warnings\n190\t- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n191\t- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n192\t- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n193\t  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n194\t  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n195\t  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n196\t  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n197\t- The checker prints them all.\n198\t\n199\t### Testing without a browser\n200\tA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n201\t- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n202\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n203\t- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n204\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n205\t- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n206\t\n207\t```js\n208\t// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\n209\tconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\n210\tfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\n211\tconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\n212\tconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\n213\tgame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\n214\tI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\n215\tif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n216\t```\n217\t\n218\t### The handoff package\n219\t`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n220\t\n221\t### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n222\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"601e35c3-b38b-43d5-9b14-109f6331e749","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:15.560Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.claude/projects/-home-micha-dev-cc-matrix-runs-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-build--20261104-050244--x8ffc89-work/9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094/tool-results/bxit8s57l.txt","content":"## Systems in more depth\n\n### Views and resolution\n- Presets: `iso` (yaw 45, pitch 30), `threequarter` (pitch 55), `topdown` (pitch 80), `brawler` (pitch 25), `side` (pitch 0).\n- `overhead` (pitch 90, scale 1) makes world x/y equal screen pixels, for shooters, puzzle and arcade games. It is not in the default view cycle.\n- Make your own with `new E.View(id, label, yaw, pitch, scale, zBoost)`.\n- `res` fixes the internal resolution with whole-number scaling. Without it, the size adapts to the window between `minH` and `maxW` × `maxH`; `portrait: { maxH }` lets the view grow on a screen held upright (a phone), so it fills the screen.\n- `res` with `expand: true` (what the starter kits use) fills any window with no bars: `res` is the least the screen shows, and `r.W` × `r.H` grows on the long side (a 16:9 monitor shows about 427×240 of `'ps1'`, an ultrawide 640×270, a phone held upright 320×690). The game's job is what fills the extra space, edge to edge, with the HUD over the world's corners (never a band of nothing). The kits show the ways. A wider screen shows more level (the adventure's neighbouring rooms, dimmed a little: they wait) or widens the play area itself (the shooter's waves spread across the whole screen). A taller one shows more world (the forest round the adventure's rooms, the RPG's sky darkening to stars), zooms a side view in (the platformer, the brawler and the RPG's battle at 1.5x, a scene's `camera.tall`, the battle's two sides closing up to fit) and lifts the level onto a band below it (the platformer's soil, the brawler's foreground wall). A painted backdrop is painted at the screen's size and zoom, its ground meeting the floor's edge. Keep prompts clear of `game.input.touchArea()` and HUDs clear of `game.cornerArea()` (the menu button stays at the top right on a phone). On a phone, make the controls smart: a button there only when it has something to do (`input.touchSet`: TALK beside someone), taps on what you see (the RPG's commands and foes), a thumb that steers from anywhere on the screen (`input.steer = 'drag'`, `input.drag()`: the shooter's ship goes 1.5x as far, so one swipe crosses it), what a phone player shouldn't have to hold done for them (the shooter fires by itself), buttons a thumb hits (44 CSS px and more: the RPG's battle pads, with AUTO), and a camera that never shows ground a fighter can't walk to (the brawler stops at a fight's lock). And keep the frame a phone's: a 120 Hz screen gives 8.3 ms, and frames that miss it (their cost, or the GPU behind them) are drawn every other refresh (`game.drawEvery`), so draw background characters at half rate (`r.actor(..., { every: 2, key })`: the adventure's townsfolk) and leave GPU lighting to a computer (its main pointer fine). Settings, Display, Show frame rate reads it on the device, and Frame rate lets the player fix it (60: every other refresh of a 120 Hz screen; Full: every refresh). `npm run test:phone` checks the kits on an emulated phone.\n- `game.views` limits view cycling (`game.nextView()`) and tells the checker which views to test.\n\n### Scenes\nA scene is `{ enter(data), exit(), update(dt), draw(r), pausable, view, views, input, res, expand, touch, camera }`.\n- `game.go('play', data)` switches before the next step; timers, tweens, scripts and particles are cleared (weather too) and the camera snaps. `{ fade: 'iris', at: hero }` (or `'wipe'`, `'mosaic'`, `'swirl'`, `'dissolve'`, `'white'`; `seconds` each way) picks the transition: an iris closing on the hero for a door, a swirl into a battle, a mosaic into a dream. A world point `at` is placed by each scene's camera; `[x, y]` is a point on the screen. Reduce motion turns each into a plain fade.\n- `pausable: true` lets `pause` (Escape, P, Start, the touch START) toggle `game.paused`: the pause menu opens (the canvas PAUSED overlay with `menu: false`) and the music is ducked. It also lets the page pause the scene by itself when it is hidden or loses focus.\n\n### Camera\n- `game.follow(hero, { z, lead })` once, or `game.focus(x, y, z)` every update.\n- `cam.bounds = v => map.bounds(v)` (or `level.bounds`) keeps it inside the level.\n- `cam.room = [w, h]` snaps the target to the center of the current room; the camera then slides, so Zelda- and Metroid-style room transitions come for free. Freeze gameplay while `cam.moving` is true.\n- The player's zoom: a scene's `camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1, tall: 1.5 }` sets how close the camera starts (`start`; `tall` on a phone held upright) and how far the player may take it (`zoom`): the mouse wheel, `-` and `=`, a two-finger pinch (both fingers moving, so a thumb on the stick isn't one) and Settings, Camera zoom. Their zoom is kept, a factor on top of the scene's, so it carries across scenes and screen shapes; each change shows `ZOOM 1.65x` at the top, the number to make `start` when tuning by feel. `zoom` may be a function of the game, a range per screen shape (the adventure's: out to .85x on a wide screen, none upright, where its forest is sized for 1x); `zoom: false` where zooming would break the game (the shooter's playfield is the screen), `wheel: false` where the wheel is the game's.\n- **The window and framing:** `cam.window = [48, 24]` is a dead zone (Super Mario World's): the camera stays still while the focus moves inside it and follows from its edge, so small steps don't jiggle the screen. `cam.snapY = true` follows a followed body's height only when it lands (its `onGround`) or nears the screen's edge, so a jump doesn't drag the camera up and down. `cam.frame([p1, p2], { margin: 24, zoom: [.5, 1] })` keeps several things in view, centred between them, zooming out (to at least `zoom[0]`) as they part and back in as they meet: two players, a boss and the hero, a race's leaders. `zoom: false` only centres.\n- Screen shake respects the player's reduced-motion setting.\n\n### Players (two to four on one machine)\n- `game.players` is `[game.input, ...the ones who joined]`. `game.input` stays player 1, so a game of one is written as before. Each other player is an input of its own with the same API (`move()`, `pressed()`, `label()`), plus `player` (2 to 4), `name` (`'P2'`), `color` (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`: blue, red, green, yellow) and `device`.\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin: (input, n) => dropIn(input) })` in a scene's `enter` lets them in: Start on a gamepad no one holds, or 2 on the keyboard. Two on one keyboard each get half: player 1 WASD with F G H R T Y (`Input.halfKeys(preset, 'left')`), player 2 the arrows with `.` `/` `,` L `;` `'` and the numpad (`'right'`), each half taking the preset's actions in turn. `game.addPlayer({ pad: 1 })` or `({ keys: 'right' })` adds one directly (tests, a versus menu); `game.removePlayer(p)`.\n- The standard features follow: each player's prompts name its own keys (`input.label`), a pause says who paused (P2 PAUSED), a lost controller names its player, the Controls screen has a page each. Recorded sessions replay every pad and both halves of the keyboard.\n- One shared screen: `cam.frame(heroes, { margin: 90 })` keeps everyone in view. A split screen: `game.splitScreen(2, { follow: [hero, hero2] })`, `layout: 'auto'` (side by side on a wide screen, stacked on a tall one, a grid for 3 or 4), `'columns'`, `'rows'` or `'grid'`. The scene's `draw(r)` runs once per viewport, its camera its own (`game.viewports[i].follow(t)`, `.focus(x, y)`), with `r.viewport` saying which (`{ i, x, y, w, h }`: draw that player's HUD there); `scene.hud(r)` then runs once over the whole screen. GPU lighting is off while split; a scene's start ends the split.\n\n### Motion and timing (tweens, scripts, paths)\nThe code every game writes by hand, in the engine and on game time: each pauses with the game, stands still in hit-stop, slows with `timeScale` and ends with the scene (unless `keep: true`), and runs the same at every refresh rate.\n- **Tweens** (Phaser's tweens, Unity's DOTween): `game.tween(target, { x: 10, y: 4 }, seconds, { ease, delay, repeat, yoyo, onDone, keep })` eases a target's numbers to new values, from where they are when its delay ends; a door sliding, a title dropping in, a coin flying to the HUD, a camera move. `ease` is a name in `E.ease` (Penner's: `linear`, `inSine`, `outBack`, `outElastic`, `outBounce`, ...; `outQuad` by default) or a function of 0..1. It replaces a timer, a start value and a lerp in every update.\n- **Scripts** (Unity's coroutines): `game.script(function* (game) { ... })` runs a generator in the update step and waits at each `yield`: a number of seconds, a condition (`E.until(fn)` or a function), a tween, another script or a generator (run and waited for), `E.all([...])`, `E.any([...])`, or nothing (one step). A wave timeline is a loop (`for (const w of WAVES) { spawn(w); yield E.any([w.time, () => !enemies.length]); }`); a cutscene is its lines in order (`talk.say(...)`, `yield E.until(() => !talk.open)`, `yield game.tween(cam, ...)`), instead of a state machine with a timer per state. It runs at once to its first `yield`; `.cancel()` stops it and runs its `finally`.\n- **Paths:** `new E.Path(points, { closed, smooth: 'catmull' })` measures a line by distance along it: `at(d)` is a point with its heading (a moving platform's place, an enemy's dive, a rail, a camera track: `d = speed * time`), `nearest(x, y)` the distance along of the nearest point (a racer's progress round the lap, for places), `draw(r)` shows it while you build.\n- **Weather:** `game.weather = { kind, amount, wind, lightning }` (rain, snow, ash, leaves, sand): drops fall through the world's height in ground views and in three layers of parallax in side and overhead views, splash or settle on the floor (`particles.ground`, the platforms' height in a side view), and lightning flashes (the player's Screen flashes setting) and thunders. It is cosmetic: its own seeded random stream, never `Math.random`, so replays and gameplay are untouched. `E.WEATHER` holds the kinds; add your own.\n- **Sprites** (frames, turns, swaps): `E.anim([...frames], fps, { loop, pingpong })` (or one sheet cut into `frames`) is played by `r.sprite(x, y, z, anim, { t })`; `rot` turns a sprite in whole pixels, cached in 16 or 32 steps as the era's games pre-rotated theirs (a ship facing its heading, a spinning blade); `scale` grows it nearest-neighbour; `E.recolor(spr, map)` is a palette swap (the second player, a stronger enemy).\n- **Wrap-around:** `E.wrap(v, lo, hi)` keeps a position on a world whose edges meet (Asteroids, Pac-Man's tunnel, a looping stage), `E.wrapDiff(a, b, size)` the short way across the seam (aim and chase across it), and `wrap: [w, h]` on `r.sprite` and `r.actor` draws a copy across each edge a thing straddles.\n\n### TileMap (ground-plane levels)\n- ASCII `rows` and a `legend`: a number is a wall type, a string is a spawn tag, `{ tile, floor, spawn }` combines them.\n- Wall `types[id] = { h, top, side, line, course, cut, cutH }`. `cut` lowers a wall when it stands between the camera and the floor.\n- `floorTex(x, y, floorTag)` colors the floor per pixel; it is baked once per view. Ready-made: `E.tex.flagstone`, `grass`, `dirt`, `water`, `planks`, `checker`, `plain`.\n- `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` opens doors or breaks walls; the floor and wall caches rebuild themselves.\n- Collision is height-aware: `collide(body)` ignores walls lower than `body.z + step`, and `groundAt(x, y, r, z)` returns the surface to stand on.\n- Things standing on a block sort after it, and `r.shadow(..., surfaceZ)` draws on top of blocks.\n- `map.block(cx, cy, on)` and `map.set(...)` bump `map.version`; a `FlowField` rebuilds at its next `update` when it changes.\n- Maps built in code: `new E.TileMap({ w, h, types })` starts as open floor; `map.fill(cx, cy, w, h, id)` puts a block of wall type `id` in (0 clears it), and `map.setFloor(cx, cy, tag, block)` tags a floor cell (`'grass'`, `'water'`) and blocks it or not.\n- Facades come from wall-type options: `windows: { w, h, step, from, lit }` (or `true`), `door: { chance, color }`, `shutters: color`, `balcony: { z, color }`, `arch: { chance }`. For anything else, `types[id].decorate(face)` paints each wall face as it is baked (so it costs nothing per frame): `face.side`, `face.u0..u1` along the wall in world units (patterns line up across cells), `face.z0..z1`, `face.at(u, z)`, `face.quad(u0, z0, u1, z1, color)`, `face.line`, `face.dot`, `face.tones`, `face.view`.\n- A big thing over several blocks (a canopy over a tower, a cart across two cells) sorts by its whole base: `r.queue(x, y, z, fn, { footprint: [x0, y0, x1, y1] })` lifts it over every block under it. `r.prop` and `r.sprite` take `footprint` too.\n\n### Heights: roofs, ledges, sight and reach\nOne rule for collision, standing, sight, reach, navigation and drawing, so a game with roofs never writes its own:\n- World units, one tile = 16, z up. A body's `z` is its feet. A cell's top is `map.height(cx, cy)`: 0 on the floor, and a wall is solid from 0 up to its `h` (one column per cell: no passages under things yet).\n- A body stands on the highest top within `z + step` under its footprint (`step` 2, `Body.step`), so a kerb of 2 is walked over and a block of 14 is jumped onto.\n- Eyes are at `z + E.HEIGHTS.eye` (30) and a target is seen at `z + E.HEIGHTS.chest` (22), the heights of a size-1.3 Humanoid. A body's own `eye` and `chest` override them: a crouching hero sets `hero.chest = 8`.\n- \"The same level\" is `|dz| <= E.HEIGHTS.level` (8) unless a call says otherwise. `E.dist` is 3D; `E.inArc` is flat unless you pass `dz`, and `E.overlap` is always flat (pair it with `E.sameLevel`).\n\nThe queries on a `TileMap` (`dir` is an angle in radians or a ground vector `[dx, dy]`; `from` is a body or any `{ x, y, z, r }`):\n- `map.ray(a, b, { eye, target })` walks every cell between two points and returns `{ clear, t, x, y, z }`, where it stops. `map.sight(a, b)` is eyes to chest; `los()` only looks across the ground (a 12-unit planter blocks it, not `sight`).\n- `map.standable(x, y, z, r)`: room to stand, held up, not in deep water.\n- `map.ledge(from, dir, { reach: 18, min: 4, rise: 82, spread: .28 })` → `{ x, y, z, rise, facing, edge: [x, y] }`: the nearest top to pull up onto, and where the hands grip.\n- `map.landing(from, dir, { min: 35, max: 83, rise: 11, drop: 18, apex: 20 })` → `{ x, y, z, d }`: a spot across a gap that a jump arc clears.\n- `map.drop(from, dir)` → `{ x, y, z, fall }`: what lies past the edge ahead (hop-downs, fall warnings, guards that won't walk off roofs).\n- `map.arcClear(a, b, apex, r)`: a parabola from feet to feet, peaking `apex` above the higher end, clears every top (jumps, throws, aerial takedowns).\n\nReach and perception:\n- `E.targets(from, list, { range, arc, facing, dz, sight: map, filter })` → `[{ target, d, dz, angle }]`, nearest first. `dz` defaults to the same level; `arc` is a half angle around `facing`. Melee auto-aim, lock-on, takedowns and interaction prompts use it.\n- `E.inArc(a, facing, b, range, halfAngle, dz)` and `E.sameLevel(a, b, tol)`.\n\nNavigation: `new E.FlowField(map, { step: 2, climb: 0, drop: 0, jump: 0 })` walks the tops a body can stand on. Next-door cells connect when their tops differ by at most `step`, or by up to `climb` going up and `drop` going down. A guard on a roof gets a field across the roofs; with `climb` and `drop` set, it chases between levels. With `jump` (the widest gap, world units) it also links roofs across a gap a jump clears, and a climb costs more than a leap, so a chase leaps rather than drop to the street and climb back. `flow.next(x, y)` → `{ x, y, z, kind: 'walk' | 'climb' | 'drop' | 'jump', edge }` says where to head: an AI on the ground presses jump for a `climb` or a `jump` once `edge` (the distance to the side of its cell it leaves by) is under its radius plus a little, and `E.Mover` does the climb or the leap.\n\n```js\nconst seen = map.sight(guard, hero) && E.inArc(guard, guard.facing, hero, 120, .7);   // eyes over planters, not through roofs\nif (input.pressed('jump')) {\n  const up = map.ledge(hero, hero.facing);   // a wall to climb in front?\n  const across = !up && map.landing(hero, hero.facing);   // or a gap to clear?\n  if (up) climbTo(up); else if (across) leapTo(across); else hero.jump(210);\n}\nconst prey = E.targets(hero, guards, { range: 22, arc: 1.2, facing: hero.facing, sight: map })[0];   // the same level by default\nif (prey && input.pressed('attack')) takedown(prey.target);\n```\n\n### PlatformMap (side-scrolling levels)\n- Tiles live on the x/z plane; the ASCII top row is the highest.\n- Kinds:\n  - `solid`;\n  - `slope`: `dir: 1` rises to the right, `-1` to the left, `from` / `to` (0..1) for gentle slopes; bodies walk up and down them and step up onto low ledges;\n  - `oneway`: you stand on it and jump up through it, and down + jump drops through;\n  - `ladder`: climbable, and you can stand on its top;\n  - `hazard`: not solid; test it with `touching(body, 'hazard')`;\n  - `deco`: drawn in the play plane;\n  - `back`: a darker background wall.\n- Styles give each tile its look: `stone` (with `moss`), `brick`, `wood`, `metal`, `ground` (grass top when open to the sky, with `flower`), `block` (bevelled), `bonus` (`?` block, `glyph` option), `pipe`, `plank`, `spikes`, `ladder`, `liquid`, `plain`; `mat` gives a solid tile's face any material (`E.MATERIALS`).\n- In `side` the level is flat like an NES or SNES game. In a tilted view with yaw 0 (`brawler` or custom), tiles show top faces and the level becomes a 2.5D diorama.\n- `move(body, dt, solids)` moves any box `{ x, z, vx, vz, w, h }` with sub-steps. It reports:\n  - `onGround` and `onOneWay`;\n  - `hitWall`: -1 left, 1 right, 0 none;\n  - `hitCeiling`;\n  - `bumped`: the tile hit from below;\n  - `ground`: the moving solid it stands on.\n- `solids` are extra boxes (moving platforms, elevators, crates); a body standing on one is carried by its `vx` / `vz`.\n\n### Platformer (side-scroller controller)\n- Defaults feel like a modern NES remaster: run 95, jump 285 (about 3 tiles), gravity 800 rising and 1150 falling, fall cap 330.\n- Forgiving controls: coyote time 0.09 s, jump buffer 0.13 s, jump cut 0.45 when jump is released early.\n- Optional moves: `airJumps` (double jump), `wallJump` (with wall slide), `dash`, `climb` speed.\n- `update(dt, level, input | intent, solids)`. The same controller runs enemies: pass `{ x, jump, jumpPressed }` from your AI.\n- `rigState(extra)` returns the object `Humanoid.update` wants, including the airborne pose.\n\n### Body (ground-plane physics)\n- Velocity with optional `friction` (per second) and `bounce` (off walls and the floor).\n- `push(ix, iy, iz)` adds knockback; `jump(v)` jumps with `gravity`.\n- It lands on the highest walkable surface: the floor, or the top of a block low enough to step onto.\n- Use it for heroes, enemies, bombs, crates, pickups and balls.\n\n### Mover and actions (third-person ground controller)\n`E.Mover` is the ground views' answer to `Platformer`: a `Body` with a third-person game's movement.\n- `const mover = new E.Mover({ x, y, z })` (the index files its members as `mover.`); each step `mover.update(dt, map, game.input)` with `input: 'THIRDPERSON'` (move, Shift sprint, Space jump and parkour, C crouch, J attack, K block, L dodge; touch buttons), or an AI's intent `{ move: [dx, dy], sprint, crouch, jumpPressed }`. Screen directions turn into ground ones through the view.\n- Walk 50, run 85, sprint 125 and sneak 32 with acceleration; a jump with coyote time and a buffer, once a press.\n- A jump press is parkour first: a ledge up to `climbMax` (82) is climbed (`climbUp`; crouch while hanging lets go), a wall up to `vault` (14) is vaulted at a run, a gap ahead is leapt (it aims for the far side's landing), a drop up to `hopMax` (48) is hopped down; otherwise it jumps.\n- Climbing any wall (`climbAny: true`, Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild): a jump press at a wall too tall for a ledge latches on, on the ground or in the air (`mover.wall` is the face, `map.wall(from, dir)`'s: its point, normal, top and facing). The stick climbs as the screen shows it (up the screen is up the wall; an AI's intent: `move: [along, -up]`), it turns outer and inner corners, a jump press leaps up (`climbJump`, which ends on the wall) or, pulled away, pushes off; near the top it pulls up (`climbUp`), at the floor it stands, crouch lets go. Lower `climbMax` (30) so roofs are climbed, not jumped onto. The STEALTH slice and the template do this.\n- Gliding (`glide: true`, Breath of the Wild, Arkham): a jump press in the air, `glideMin` (24) over the ground, opens a sail: it sinks at `glideSink`, flies at `glideSpeed`, takes no fall damage, rises over an updraft (`lift`: a speed, or `m => inUpdraft(m) ? 40 : 0`), and closes on a press, on landing, or at a wall (a climber takes hold). `mover.gliding` says so; the rig hangs under it with its hands up, so draw the sail over them (the template does).\n- Stamina (off until you set `staminaMax`; the template's is 100): climbing, climb jumps, gliding and sprinting spend `mover.stamina` (`tire`), standing refills it (`staminaRegen`); at 0 it lets go of the wall, the glide closes and it can't sprint (`mover.exhausted`) until a third is back. Show it with `ui.hud({ bars: [{ label: 'Stamina', value: mover.stamina, max: mover.staminaMax }] })`.\n- Falls: past `rollAt` (26) at speed it rolls, past `hurtAt` (72) it sets `hurt`. `lastSafe` and `respawn()` bring it back from water and pits (`inHazard`).\n- `mover.rigState(extra)` feeds `rig.update`: its crouch (a sneaking gait), sprint lean, air, landing, or the playing action's pose.\n- Actions (`E.ACTIONS`): `climbUp` (played backwards to climb down), `vault`, `hopDown`, `roll` (invulnerable in its middle), `getUp`, and `takedown`, a paired action that moves two bodies: `mover.play('takedown', { other: guard, aerial, map })`. While `mover.action` plays it moves the body instead of physics; `act.beat`, `act.u`, `act.canCancel`, `act.invulnerable`, `act.otherState()` for the victim's rig. Your own: `E.ACTIONS.slide = { beats: [['go', .4]], setup(A) {}, at(A, beat, u) { return { x, y, z, state } } }`.\n\n### Perception (sight, hearing, suspicion)\n- `E.sees(guard, hero, { map, range: 120, fov: 1.6, eye })` → `{ seen, d, angle, exposure }`: inside the cone, and three points on the hero (feet, chest, head) checked against every top with `map.ray`. Crouching shows less and lower; `hero.exposure` (0..1) is cover you set: tall grass, shadow.\n- `E.hears(guard, { x, y, z, loud: 80 }, map)`: 1 at the noise, 0 at its radius, halved through a wall.\n- `const mind = new E.Suspicion({ rise, decay, notice, search, alert, forget })`; each step `mind.update(dt, E.sees(...), hero)` → `'calm' | 'notice' | 'search' | 'alert'` (it rises faster the nearer and more exposed the hero is), `mind.last` (where it last saw or heard), `mind.changed`. An alert guard that loses the hero searches for `forget` seconds; `mind.hear(strength, at)` notices a noise.\n- Drawing: `r.sightCone(guard, { map, range, fov, color, alpha })` (cut short by walls) and `r.marker(x, y, z, { label, color })`, an objective marker that becomes an arrow at the screen's edge.\n\n### Attack (melee timing)\n- `new E.Attack(spec)` owns the wind-up, active and recover timers of one move; the spec is also the Humanoid attack spec.\n- `start()` begins a swing (pass `true` to chain from recover into a combo); `update(dt)` returns the phase that just began, which is where swing sounds go.\n- `hits(targets, test, fn)` calls `fn` once per target per swing while active. `state` feeds `rig.update({ attack })`.\n- `E.Combo([spec1, spec2, spec3], { window })` chains attacks: `press()` flows into the next hit during recover (or shortly after), otherwise restarts. `E.knockback(from, target, speed, up)` pushes the target away.\n- **Special moves** (input sequences): `game.input.motion('236', 'attack', { facing: hero.facing })` is true on the step a fireball's motion ends: down, down-forward, forward, then the button (numpad notation, as fighting games write moves: `'623'` a dragon punch, `'41236'` a half circle, `'66'` a dash), mirrored when the fighter faces left; the button may land with the last direction, and the whole move must come within `window` seconds (.45). `input.sequence(['up', 'up', 'down', 'down', 'left', 'right', 'left', 'right', 'kick', 'attack'], { window: 3 })` reads any run of inputs (a cheat code, a rhythm pattern); a step joins actions with `+`. Both read `input.history`, the last second of directions (numpad digits) and presses, which an input display can draw.\n\n### Bullets and patterns\n- A pool on the `'ground'` plane (top-down) or the `'side'` plane.\n- Bullets die on walls with a small spark; `hit(targets, fn, team)` tests circles (side targets use their box center).\n- `burst(base, E.pattern.spread(...))` fires a pattern: `dir`, `aim`, `aimSide`, `spread`, `ring`.\n- Bullets glow under GPU lighting. Set `sprite` for custom art and `pierce` to pass through targets.\n\n### Humanoid\n- Proportions: `legUpper legLower hipZ torso shoulderHalf headR armUpper armLower limbW torsoW`.\n- Style: `lean hunch stride swing speedRef`.\n- Gear: `weapon` (`'sword'`, `'gun'`, `'staff'`), `hat` (`'cap'`, `'pointed'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`, `'crown'`), `hood`, `cape`, `eyeGlow`, `colors`.\n- `update(dt, { x, y, z, vx, vy, facing, dash, hurt, air, point, attack })`.\n- Attacks:\n  - You own the timing: move `phase` from `'wind'` to `'active'` to `'recover'` with your own timers, and pass `u` (0 to 1) within the phase.\n  - `spec.a0` / `a1` are hand angles relative to facing; `z0` / `z1` are heights.\n  - `spin: true` swings a full circle, `kick: true` swings the right foot, `blade: 0` hides the smear.\n- `hand()` and `tip()` give world points for spawning bullets or sparks; `worldOffset(p)` gives where any point in the rig's own frame (a joint in `J`) sits from its feet. `debug(r)` draws the skeleton.\n\n### Audio\n- Browsers start sound only after the first key press or click; the engine waits for it.\n- `sfx(name)` ignores the same sound fired twice in 35 ms, and varies the pitch slightly so repeats don't sound robotic.\n- `music(song)` loops tracks of its own step notation. Every track loops on its own length, so short drum or bass loops can run under long melodies.\n- Built-in songs: `title`, `adventure`, `dungeon`, `boss`, `victory` (plays once). The songs module's songbook has more by mood (`E.Songbook.find('town')`) and jingles.\n- The music clock (the full engine): a song's place is game time (`audio.pos`), not the sound's, so beats land on the same update step headless and on any device, and the sound is held to that clock (it moves its notes to meet it). Time gameplay to `audio.pos`, `audio.at(step, fn)` and `audio.onBeat(fn)`, never to the sound. A song with `sync: true` pauses with the game.\n- Dynamic music: one song in layers rather than cuts. Name the tracks, start the extra ones with `on: false`, and bring them in with `audio.layer('drums', 1, .5)` (the stealth slice's chase); `audio.tempo(bpm)` for a hurry-up; `audio.sting(song, { at: 'bar' })` for a jingle on the next bar; `music(song, { fade: 1 })` to crossfade.\n\n### Text and UI\n- `E.font` is a 5x7 proportional pixel font with lower case (the old 3x5 font is `font: 'tiny'`), with `scale`, `align`, `wrap`, outline, drop `shadow` and `gradient`; `E.font.title` draws extruded gradient logos.\n- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n  - light that wraps around characters;\n  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n\n### Errors and warnings\n- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n- The checker prints them all.\n\n### Testing without a browser\nA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n\n```js\n// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\nconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\nconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\nconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\nif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n```\n\n### The handoff package\n`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n\n### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n","numLines":222,"startLine":1,"totalLines":222}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01UGrfiJh34yBZJ8Hmoafg83","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrcZmfQVPc3vV3dBhbd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019dqBEA1qcWmZKdHspcGS8k","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 402,660p AI_GUIDE.md | grep -n \"Gauntlet\\|Twin-stick\\|^###\\|^\\*\\*\" | head -60","description":"Find recipe section boundaries in AI_GUIDE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15194,"cache_read_input_tokens":125170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15194},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"6c2ea88e-33f6-473e-9b58-4584e2bc027b","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:18.626Z","request_id":"req_011CghrcZWXuG4g4MBByYmvQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_019dqBEA1qcWmZKdHspcGS8k":{"command":"sed -n 402,660p AI_GUIDE.md | grep -n \"Gauntlet\\|Twin-stick\\|^###\\|^\\*\\*\" | head -60","description":"Find recipe section boundaries in AI_GUIDE"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019dqBEA1qcWmZKdHspcGS8k","type":"tool_result","content":"4:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n20:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n26:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n31:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n37:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n42:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n49:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n55:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n60:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n64:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n66:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n73:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n79:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n84:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n92:**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The click module (the full engine) keeps the rooms, the walking, the verbs, the inventory and the rules; the game paints the rooms and writes the puzzles.\n97:**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, { zoom: 2 })`, its expression by `rig.update(dt, { expr: 'smile' })`), and the story module's nodes and choices for the script, its flags for the routes. `story.log` is the backlog (`{ who, name, text }`: draw the last lines when the player asks), `story.auto = 2` turns pages by themselves after two seconds, `story.skip = true` types and turns them at once (choices still wait). Save with `game.saves` (`story.state()` in its data).\n99:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (the full engine, after the grid module) keeps the rules; the game draws.\n105:**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n111:**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n115:**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n134:**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n140:**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n147:**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n154:**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n161:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n163:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n165:**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n167:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n172:**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n174:**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n178:**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n183:**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n187:**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n189:**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n191:**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n193:**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n195:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n197:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n199:**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n233:**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n239:**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n241:**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n243:**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n245:**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"076820b7-79ef-40ec-a62b-8bf09d1ceb2d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:18.641Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"4:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n20:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n26:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n31:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n37:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n42:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n49:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n55:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n60:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n64:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n66:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n73:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n79:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n84:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n92:**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The click module (the full engine) keeps the rooms, the walking, the verbs, the inventory and the rules; the game paints the rooms and writes the puzzles.\n97:**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, { zoom: 2 })`, its expression by `rig.update(dt, { expr: 'smile' })`), and the story module's nodes and choices for the script, its flags for the routes. `story.log` is the backlog (`{ who, name, text }`: draw the last lines when the player asks), `story.auto = 2` turns pages by themselves after two seconds, `story.skip = true` types and turns them at once (choices still wait). Save with `game.saves` (`story.state()` in its data).\n99:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (the full engine, after the grid module) keeps the rules; the game draws.\n105:**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n111:**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n115:**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n134:**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n140:**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n147:**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n154:**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n161:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n163:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n165:**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n167:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n172:**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n174:**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n178:**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n183:**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n187:**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n189:**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n191:**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n193:**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n195:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n197:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n199:**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n233:**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n239:**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n241:**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n243:**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n245:**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019dqBEA1qcWmZKdHspcGS8k","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrcnqTcWncvYKpnCdcE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LpqY5qGe1LYyGZAaxUJEKF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 575,590p AI_GUIDE.md; echo -----; sed -n 606,660p AI_GUIDE.md","description":"Read Gauntlet/twin-stick recipes and Emberdeep lessons"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5445,"cache_read_input_tokens":140364,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5445},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"0793a102-2da9-42cc-897d-f0b67a30cd2a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:21.472Z","request_id":"req_011CghrcnYMYJ389miDHgLTZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01LpqY5qGe1LYyGZAaxUJEKF":{"command":"sed -n 575,590p AI_GUIDE.md; echo -----; sed -n 606,660p AI_GUIDE.md","description":"Read Gauntlet/twin-stick recipes and Emberdeep lessons"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LpqY5qGe1LYyGZAaxUJEKF","type":"tool_result","content":"**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n- Waves spawn from the room's edges by a timeline; grunts walk straight at the hero, others wander, hulks can't be killed, humans to rescue for points. `cam.shake` and `game.freeze(.03)` on every kill keep it loud; `E.Arcade` keeps the score, the lives and the table.\n\n**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\n- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n\n**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n- Up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors.\n- Generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero (one field for all, recomputed when a hero crosses a cell), drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }` (kept images) to stay fast.\n\n**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n\n**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n-----\n## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n`examples/emberdeep.html` is the engine's signature game: an endless Diablo-style hack-and-slash of about 2 MB of code, built from some fifty source files. Its structure (`src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`) scales to any large game.\n\n- **A spine, then content.** A small core defines:\n  - the shared state;\n  - one damage pipeline for every unit;\n  - a handful of effect verbs (projectile, nova, ground area, telegraph, strike, meteor, chain, beam, wave, pull);\n  - the hero's action model;\n  - the monster runtime;\n  - the level builder;\n  - the UI kit.\n  Content never edits the core.\n- **Registries make a language.** Every part is registered: `def('skills' | 'archetypes' | 'affixes' | 'bosses' | 'patterns' | 'itemBases' | 'powers' | 'mechanics' | 'themes' | 'layouts' | 'npcs', id, spec)`. Parts refer to each other by id and combine freely. A monster is an archetype × an element × affixes × depth scaling × a palette. A level is a layout × a theme × mechanics × a monster pool. A boss is a body × an element × patterns.\n- **An event bus instead of hooks everywhere.** Legendary powers, passive keystones, mechanics and tips listen to `hit`, `kill`, `skill`, `dodge`, `step` and `draw` events and check whether they apply. Adding a power never touches the combat code.\n- **Procedural forever.** A recipe per depth draws from the registries. Past the hand-planned depths it composes new combinations in a fixed order (every pair of mechanics, then every triple), recolors themes by depth, varies packs (elements, shared affixes, giants, swarms) and assembles bosses from a pattern library. Both sides of the power curve grow exponentially, the monsters' a little faster.\n- **New animation on the rigs.** Post-process the Humanoid's joints after `rig.update`:\n  - a dodge roll turns every joint around the hips;\n  - drinking re-solves one arm with `E.ik3`;\n  - held tools are drawn from the hand and elbow joints.\n  New creature rigs (IK-legged spiders, a path-following serpent) are posed in 3D and projected with `view.p`.\n- **Test like a player.**\n  - An autopilot drives the hero through a virtual input for balance runs and an attract-mode demo.\n  - Scripted headless playtests (`tools/ed-play.mjs`) and a smoke test over every scene and depth (`tools/ed-smoke.mjs`) keep a big game honest.\n  - A Gallery scene plays every skill and monster for visual review.\n\n## Where to find more: animation and examples\nThe engine files carry the engine; the repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge) also holds a store of motion and a whole game of worked examples. Take from it rather than inventing a walk cycle or a spider from nothing, but take only what a game needs: the store's files are large (a clip set is 75-820 KB, each motion-capture subject about 2.5 MB), so search them and never read one whole. All of it runs at https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs.\n\n**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n- **Find** a clip by what it does. The curated sets have catalogs, each clip's name with its tags and a description: `src/mocap/catalogs/quaternius.json` (88 clips: idles, walks, runs, jumps, sword, gun, shield, magic and fist attacks, dodges, hits, deaths, sitting, talking, work), `mesh2motion.json` (177: idles, attacks, emotes and gestures, dodges, climbing, sneaking, zombies, chores) and `cmu.json` (60 moments cut from motion capture: gaits, fights and kicks, dodges, falls and get-ups, chores, monsters and zombies acted by people). The motion-capture takes are in a ledger, one a line with its category, length and description: `node tools/cmu.mjs ledger kick` searches it, or grep `src/mocap/catalogs/cmu-takes.tsv`.\n- **Take** the clips you want into a small set of your own: `node tools/anim-set.mjs src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js --clips Idle_Loop,10_01 --name MINE --out mine.js` (the curated sets are `src/mocap/sets/<set>.js`; take `NN_xx` is in `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_<NN>.js`). A clip copied by hand also needs its set's preamble (its `sources`, the bodies it was measured on), so use the tool when you can run commands.\n- **Play** it: load `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js` and the set after the engine, then `const lib = Mocap.load(MOCAP.MINE); Mocap.drive(rig, lib);` once, and each step `rig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('Idle_Loop'), t)` before `rig.update`. `rig.mocapW` (0 to 1) fades a clip in and out over the rig's own animation, `rig.mocapMask = 'upper'` plays only the chest, head and arms over the rig's own walk, and `lib.moveAt(clip, t)` says how far a travelling clip has carried the body.\n- **Edit** it: a clip is text. Change a key pose's numbers to fix or vary it (the Mocap Lab's AI panel shows a clip as text and plays an edit).\n\n**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n\n**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n\n**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n\n**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.\n\n## Performance\n- **Rigs:** projection costs microseconds. The outline composite is the expensive part, so crowds should use `outline: false`.\n- **Walls, floors and PlatformMap chunks** are baked once per view.\n- **Canvas lighting** scans pixels inside each light, so keep radii sensible or use GPU lighting.\n- **Particles** are capped at 900 (`game.particles.max`) and bullets at 800 per pool.\n- **Crowd separation:** use `E.SpatialHash`, not all-pairs checks.\n- The stress test (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-test) benchmarks a device and prints a copyable report.\n\n## Checklist before handing a game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge`, holding only your game (no leftover starter menu or slices): between `GAME START` and `GAME END` in a single file, `game/game.js` in the handoff package. No libraries or network requests.\n- A title scene, a gameplay scene with `pausable: true`, and a game-over or win path back to the title.\n- No pause menu, settings screen, key rebinding or full-screen code of your own: they're standard. Your settings, names and menu items go in `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; prompts come from `game.input.label(action)`.\n- The camera follows the player and the level is visible. The HUD uses screen pixels (`r.text`, `r.overlay`), anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`, or for a modern game `E.Shell`'s HTML pieces (`ui.hud` every step). With `expand: true`, look at it on a wide monitor and on a phone both ways (`tools/check.mjs` takes `--size 390x844@3`).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fe4bfc6-ee9b-4f5a-880a-60c3aeac1094","uuid":"46da88b7-7d50-4c75-9979-f4a60831bdd6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:04:21.484Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n- Waves spawn from the room's edges by a timeline; grunts walk straight at the hero, others wander, hulks can't be killed, humans to rescue for points. `cam.shake` and `game.freeze(.03)` on every kill keep it loud; `E.Arcade` keeps the score, the lives and the table.\n\n**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\n- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n\n**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n- Up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors.\n- Generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero (one field for all, recomputed when a hero crosses a cell), drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }` (kept images) to stay fast.\n\n**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n\n**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n-----\n## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n`examples/emberdeep.html` is the engine's signature game: an endless Diablo-style hack-and-slash of about 2 MB of code, built from some fifty source files. Its structure (`src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`) scales to any large game.\n\n- **A spine, then content.** A small core defines:\n  - the shared state;\n  - one damage pipeline for every unit;\n  - a handful of effect verbs (projectile, nova, ground area, telegraph, strike, meteor, chain, beam, wave, pull);\n  - the hero's action model;\n  - the monster runtime;\n  - the level builder;\n  - the UI kit.\n  Content never edits the core.\n- **Registries make a language.** Every part is registered: `def('skills' | 'archetypes' | 'affixes' | 'bosses' | 'patterns' | 'itemBases' | 'powers' | 'mechanics' | 'themes' | 'layouts' | 'npcs', id, spec)`. Parts refer to each other by id and combine freely. A monster is an archetype × an element × affixes × depth scaling × a palette. A level is a layout × a theme × mechanics × a monster pool. A boss is a body × an element × patterns.\n- **An event bus instead of hooks everywhere.** Legendary powers, passive keystones, mechanics and tips listen to `hit`, `kill`, `skill`, `dodge`, `step` and `draw` events and check whether they apply. Adding a power never touches the combat code.\n- **Procedural forever.** A recipe per depth draws from the registries. Past the hand-planned depths it composes new combinations in a fixed order (every pair of mechanics, then every triple), recolors themes by depth, varies packs (elements, shared affixes, giants, swarms) and assembles bosses from a pattern library. Both sides of the power curve grow exponentially, the monsters' a little faster.\n- **New animation on the rigs.** Post-process the Humanoid's joints after `rig.update`:\n  - a dodge roll turns every joint around the hips;\n  - drinking re-solves one arm with `E.ik3`;\n  - held tools are drawn from the hand and elbow joints.\n  New creature rigs (IK-legged spiders, a path-following serpent) are posed in 3D and projected with `view.p`.\n- **Test like a player.**\n  - An autopilot drives the hero through a virtual input for balance runs and an attract-mode demo.\n  - Scripted headless playtests (`tools/ed-play.mjs`) and a smoke test over every scene and depth (`tools/ed-smoke.mjs`) keep a big game honest.\n  - A Gallery scene plays every skill and monster for visual review.\n\n## Where to find more: animation and examples\nThe engine files carry the engine; the repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge) also holds a store of motion and a whole game of worked examples. Take from it rather than inventing a walk cycle or a spider from nothing, but take only what a game needs: the store's files are large (a clip set is 75-820 KB, each motion-capture subject about 2.5 MB), so search them and never read one whole. All of it runs at https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs.\n\n**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n- **Find** a clip by what it does. The curated sets have catalogs, each clip's name with its tags and a description: `src/mocap/catalogs/quaternius.json` (88 clips: idles, walks, runs, jumps, sword, gun, shield, magic and